VisWiz
======

VisWiz is a dataset-first visualization plugin for WordPress, with optional WooCommerce data sources and an investigative graph explorer.

The core design rule is:

> **Datasets own data. Visualizations render data.**

A dataset is the canonical source of truth and can be reused by multiple visualizations. Editing data does not require maintaining duplicate copies inside individual visualizations.

Project status
--------------

VisWiz 2.x is in active stabilization. The storage model, revision system, WordPress integration, WooCommerce querying and graph data model have been substantially rebuilt. Current work is focused on making visualization creation and dataset editing/import faster, clearer and more robust for everyday use.

The plugin header and the latest GitHub Release are the source of truth for the current version.

Requirements
------------

- WordPress 6.5 or newer
- PHP 8.1 or newer
- WooCommerce only when WooCommerce live data or snapshots are used

Core workflow
-------------

1. **Create or import a dataset.**
2. **Choose a visualization renderer and connect it to the dataset** — or use a saved WooCommerce live query where supported.
3. **Configure display and interaction options.**
4. **Publish with the VisWiz block or shortcode.**

Datasets
--------

VisWiz currently supports these canonical dataset schemas:

- Categorical
- Time series
- X/Y points
- Geographic points
- Progress
- Node graph
- Diagram / sections

Dataset management includes:

- targeted row, node and relation editing
- dataset search and schema filtering
- graph integrity validation
- graph preview
- JSON import/export
- dataset duplication and safe deletion
- revision history and restore
- WooCommerce snapshot replacement for row-based datasets
- optimistic concurrency checks to prevent stale editors from silently overwriting newer data

Graph relations use immutable node UUIDs rather than editable slugs, so node renaming does not break relation identity.

Visualizations
--------------

Available renderer families currently include:

- Pie
- Bar
- Column
- Line
- Area
- Scatter
- Progress
- Counter
- Timeline
- Map
- Graph
- Flow diagram
- Org chart
- Diagram

Renderer compatibility is validated against the connected dataset schema.

Graph exploration
-----------------

The graph renderer is intended for exploratory and investigative datasets. Current interaction features include:

- text search
- type and property facets
- relation filtering
- multi-select graph facets
- zoom, reset and pointer panning
- full-screen mode
- node images and type/subtype labels
- node detail dialogs and image galleries
- related-node navigation
- property views
- one-hop and two-hop **Focus on connections** exploration

WooCommerce
-----------

WooCommerce can be used either as a saved live data source or to replace a row-based dataset with a snapshot.

The WooCommerce query layer uses WooCommerce CRUD/query APIs rather than direct order-table queries and declares HPOS compatibility. Supported query dimensions include:

- revenue, order count and item quantity
- total, month, product and order-status grouping
- relative or fixed date periods
- created, paid or completed date basis
- product and category filters
- refund deduction
- gross, item-net and item-gross revenue bases

Live results are cached server-side and public requests execute only saved visualization queries.

Publishing and runtime
----------------------

- Dynamic Gutenberg block: `viswiz/visualization`
- Shortcode: `[viswiz_visualization id="123"]`
- Legacy VisWiz shortcodes resolve through saved visualization IDs
- Front-end assets are loaded only when a VisWiz visualization is rendered
- Runtime renderers are local; no D3 or third-party visualization CDN is required
- Public visualization payloads are exposed through the VisWiz v2 REST API

Data integrity and migration
----------------------------

VisWiz 2 uses dedicated v2 tables for datasets, rows, graph nodes, graph relations and revisions.

Normal editing uses targeted writes. Full replacement is reserved for explicit import/replace/restore operations and is transactional. Every data mutation creates a recoverable dataset revision.

The 1.x-to-2.x migration writes into separate v2 tables and deliberately leaves legacy tables untouched as a rollback/reference source until migration has been verified on the real installation.

Updates
-------

Releases are published through GitHub with an installable `viswiz-{version}.zip` asset. The updater supplies release metadata and packages to WordPress; WordPress core remains authoritative for the per-plugin automatic-update preference shown on the Installed Plugins screen.

Development and validation
--------------------------

The repository includes automated checks for:

- PHP syntax on supported PHP versions
- JavaScript syntax
- PHPUnit domain and architecture tests
- block/composer JSON validation
- whitespace errors
- WordPress + MySQL integration smoke tests
- WooCommerce installation and query smoke tests
- v1-to-v2 migration behavior
- graph UUID/relation identity and optimistic concurrency
- graph rendering compatibility and interaction regressions

Documentation
-------------

- `docs/PROJECT_REFERENCE.md` — authoritative upstream documentation and project engineering baseline
- `docs/USABILITY_BACKLOG.md` — current stabilization and usability priorities

Full user and developer documentation will be written after the editor, import workflow and front-end interaction model have stabilized.